# decision Contact delivery is stateless, layered and ordered

`contact` resolves intent through the same router as `who`, then selects the first declared
contact whose kind has a driver. Resolution checks the consumer's PATH plugin before a built-in,
then consent-gated declared invocations, then an instruction. A delivery emits exactly one stdout
record containing the agent, kind, chosen driver, remote identifier, and state; git-a2a writes no
conversation or delivery state.

# decision A2A uses the native version 1.0 JSON-RPC binding

The A2A driver sends JSON-RPC 2.0 method `SendMessage` with the `A2A-Version: 1.0` header and a
version 1.0 user `Message`. `--wait` uses `SendStreamingMessage` and consumes its SSE events.
Both paths report the returned task id/state, or the returned message id when no task exists.
A non-2xx response uses the shared remote-response sanitizer. HTML identified by Content-Type or
a leading `<` after trimming is fully suppressed and reported with its byte count; other bodies
have control characters removed, whitespace collapsed, and are limited to 200 Unicode characters.
The remote body is diagnostic data, never terminal control or an unbounded error payload.

# decision GitHub issues prefer the authenticated CLI

The `github-issue` driver invokes `gh issue create` when `gh` is on PATH. Only when it is absent
does the driver call the GitHub Issues REST API, using `GH_TOKEN` then `GITHUB_TOKEN` and
`GITHUB_API_URL` for GitHub Enterprise. A present but failing `gh` is reported, not silently
replayed through REST, so one request cannot create two issues.

# decision Forge issue drivers share degradation and request construction

GitHub, GitLab, and Gitea-family issue contacts share bounded title/body construction, label
handling, deep-link rendering, and remote-response sanitization. Each prefers its native CLI;
only an absent CLI may fall back to that forge's REST API and consumer-local environment token.
A present but failing CLI is never replayed through REST. GitLab project paths are encoded as one
path component so nested groups survive. Gitea, Forgejo, and Codeberg use the same Gitea API kind.

# decision Passive contacts print an actionable record

`url`, Mattermost, Slack, Discord, Telegram, and Teams do not cause external delivery.
Their drivers emit a one-line instruction naming the declared destination and referring to the
supplied message without copying multiline message content into stdout. Because instruction
kinds are real drivers, their declaration order relative to active drivers is preserved.

# decision Email uses consumer transport and owner coordinates

The `email` driver prefers consumer-installed `sendmail` and pipes one RFC 5322 message with
`-i -t`. Otherwise `GITA2A_SMTP_URL=smtps://user@host[:port]` plus
`GITA2A_SMTP_PASSWORD` supplies consumer-owned implicit-TLS SMTP credentials. Otherwise it
returns the existing instruction. The owner supplies only `address` and optional
`subject-prefix`; neither can inject a header. `--dry-run` remains an instruction, and MCP may
deliver because email is an external delivery rather than owner-declared local execution.

# decision PATH plugins implement the open contact vocabulary

Before any built-in, contact looks up exactly `git-a2a-contact-<kind>` on the consumer's PATH;
kind values containing path separators never participate in discovery. Protocol v1 is one JSON
request on stdin and one JSON record on stdout. Exit 0 delivers, 1 fails, 2 refuses; stderr is
prefixed and forwarded, and the process is killed after 60 seconds. The record names
`plugin:<executable-name>`. MCP delegates to this same resolver.

# decision Declared invocations are instructions until the consumer consents

The `http` and `exec` drivers substitute only the normative closed placeholder set. HTTP executes
only for an origin in the consumer manifest's `settings.contact.allow-http`; exec executes only
for an allowlisted bare binary that resolves on consumer PATH. Exec is argv-only with inherited
consumer environment and no expansion or shell. MCP refuses declared exec before plugin or
built-in resolution. Without consent both render the exact request or argv/stdin as a successful
instruction. HTTP failures use the shared bounded remote-response sanitizer.

# test Contact drivers

- An httptest A2A endpoint observes the exact method, protocol header, user role, and text part.
- Streaming consumes every SSE event and reports the final task state once.
- Delivery leaves the consumer repository byte-for-byte unchanged.
- A fake `gh` receives the repository, first non-empty message line as title, full message on
  stdin, and every declared label; REST fallback is exercised by an httptest server.
- TLS httptest servers exercise GitLab v4 nested-project encoding and Gitea v1 label payloads,
  success and sanitized 4xx responses; fake `glab` and `tea` prove CLI preference.
- Plugin tests cover JSON round-trip, refusal, timeout, prefixed stderr, CLI/MCP parity, and a
  Windows `.cmd` PATH stub. Declared invocation tests prove context escaping, allowlist gating,
  argv-not-shell behavior, and MCP exec refusal.
- A full CLI end-to-end test publishes a JSON-body `http` contact in a local bare owner repository,
  then runs `add → fetch → consumer consent → contact` into a TLS httptest endpoint and decodes the
  received JSON after quote/bracket injection through `{message}`. This lifecycle layer is
  mandatory: validator-only and driver-only tests can both pass while their composition through a
  fetched manifest is broken, which is how literal JSON braces escaped the first review.
- A fake SMTP conversation proves auth, envelope, RFC 5322 headers, and body; a sendmail stub
  proves argv and stdin. Neither test reads a real credential or opens the network.
